const faqs=[
 {question:"What is bias in a customer interview?",answer:"Bias is a systematic influence that pushes the interview or its interpretation away from the participant’s actual experience. It can enter through participant selection, question wording, interviewer reactions, solution pitching, memory, social desirability or selective analysis."},
 {question:"What is a leading question in customer research?",answer:"A leading question suggests the preferred answer or embeds an assumption. ‘Wouldn’t this save you time?’ leads the participant toward agreement. A more neutral question is ‘Where does time go in the current process?’"},
 {question:"How do you avoid confirmation bias in customer interviews?",answer:"Write assumptions and disconfirming evidence before interviews, use neutral questions, interview people beyond friendly contacts, record contradictions, separate observations from interpretations and have another person review the guide, transcripts or coding."},
 {question:"Can the founder conduct customer interviews objectively?",answer:"A founder can learn a great deal from interviews, but personal investment may affect questions, reactions and interpretation. Training, a consistent guide, transcripts, explicit evidence criteria and independent review reduce the risk. An independent interviewer can be useful when candour or objectivity is especially important."},
 {question:"Should you record and transcribe customer interviews?",answer:"With appropriate participant information and consent, transcripts create a reviewable evidence trail and support systematic comparison. They reduce reliance on memory and selective notes, but they do not remove bias from the questions, sample or interpretation."},
 {question:"How can you tell whether an interview question is biased?",answer:"Ask whether the wording reveals the desired answer, assumes the problem exists, asks two things at once, requires speculation, uses unfamiliar jargon or pressures the participant to appear helpful. Pilot the guide and inspect whether questions repeatedly produce agreement without concrete examples."}
];
